[[ch02-introduction-vote-bank]]
== New Democracy Introduction: Pieces Not Ideology

New Democracy is pieces not ideology.

The biggest innovation or piece of New Democracy is replacing rule of law with experimental society. Replacing rule of law is taboo today and not a good innovation to introduce New Democracy with so this introduction is about vote banking. Vote banking is just one of many ideas or innovations that make up New Democracy. 

New Democracy is engineering represented by a large collection of social innovations. Vote banking is one such innovation. As an innovation vote banking can be used independently of any other part of New Democracy. New Democracy is not an ideology, it is just a collection of experiments.

In its simplest implementation vote banking is a deposit of a voting currency just as a bank is a deposit of financial currency. How this innovation is implemented is engineering.

Why vote banking?

1. Truth: the truth of 24/7 tallying of deposited votes replaces polling.
2. Convenience: the convenience of 24/7 voting just like we have any-time banking today.
3. No recounts: no recounts necessary as votes are always tallied.
4. Familiarity: the familiarity of vote banks as financial banks.

Vote banking in 1789 would have made no sense. Very few people had access to a bank. Today, everyone has access to a bank. We can trust bank deposits so we can trust vote deposits.

What if someone doesn't have access to vote banking technoglogy? Easy, a tiered level of voting can be implemented. Vote banking as the common case and traditional vote casting as the fall back. Again, innovation is not ideology and so casting votes is not excluded.

You, or anyone else could stop here and run with vote banking as an innovation without knowing anything else about New Democracy. This is why New Democracy is pieces not ideology. There is no socialism, communism, or other 'ism' for New Democracy, just innovation and engineering. Feel free to stop reading here: implement vote banking and never look back. 

Again, the idea of vote banking just stated is a stand-alone piece of innovation that anyone can run with and do so without knowing anything else about New Democracy.

Vote banking in New Democracy has many additional innovations in addition to depositing votes. From here on these additions are discussed.

Vote Banking: Content Banking

Content banking is when vote banking includes detailed information about candidates and ballot measures identical to States send out voting pamphlets prior to elections. The huge benefit here is that this information is made available 24/7. Oftentimes voter educational pamphlets show up just a few short weeks before an election. Vote banking means information about a candidate or ballot measure lives side-by-side with the vote process available 24/7. Compare this to vote casting where on election day votes only has a simple ballot of names and no content.

Vote Banking: Public Voting

New Democracy requires all votes be made public. This is one of the trust building measures in New Democracy. New Democracy is about rebuilding trust in society that has decayed due to technology. Public voting is something most people will be against today. New Democracy is a four-hundred year plan and public voting is one of those ideas that will take time, generations even. Public voting depends on the following innovations of corruption voting and proxy voting to manage public trust.


Vote Banking: Corruption Voting

Wouldn't you rather receive in your pocket the millions and billions of dollars spent on election ads rather than watching crappy ads? This is the idea behind corruption voting. Corruption voting is dependent on public voting. Corruption voting means someone can buy your vote with cold hard cash. The requirements are:

1. The dollar amount is made public.
2. Who is paying for the vote is made public.
3. The option to be paid is public and displayed as yes/no.

Millions of people will opt to be paid. That's the point. Corruption voting provides a certain amount of protection for public voting. Say, for example, an employer wants all its employees to vote a certain way. The employer could simply pay for the votes in full public view.

One purpose of corruption voting is to break up the rich as a monolith faction that we see in 2026. The rich will compete with each other by paying for votes. This will pit the rich against each other in public. This is a nullification concept where corruption is not eliminated, an impossibility, but nullified by pitting the rich against each other. Today the rich are aligned.

Vote Banking: Proxy Voting

Proxy voting is dependent on public voting but is not dependent on corruption voting. However, New Democracy relies on proxy voting to shape public trust and avoid some of the dangers of personal persecution of a public vote.

Proxy voting is simply designating someone or some group to represent your vote. This means vote banking needs to include the proxy person or group designation.

Proxy voting works hand-in-hand with corruption voting. Rich people can spread their money out by negotiating with a proxy that can represent millions and then it is up to the proxy to distribute the cash.

Proxy voting allows someone to change votes by switching proxies. If one proxy is offering more money than another then the new proxy can be selected.

Proxy voting gets complicated quickly as to proxy groups and any regulations. These will not be covered in more depth here except for how New Democracy uses proxy voting.

New Democracy is founded upon mesh democracy where the unit of public record is the micro-governing organization, or MGO. MGOs are fixed size groups of thirty people. The MGO internal governing is 100% at the discretion of the MGO members and within the MGO voting can be one person, one vote. However, in New Democracy public voting is by MGO only: one MGO, one vote. The MGO as a group of thirty people must decide how to vote as one in public. This is the first level of trust voting.

The second level of trust voting is the MGO voting in New Democracy. The vote bank only registers MGOs. MGO groups are projects. Project contracts are the mesh in mesh democracy where MGOs are the mesh nodes connected by meshes of project contracts. Groups of MGOs will represent certain material and service blocks the same as corporations do today. There are no corporations in New Democracy because there is no rule of law, only an experimental society of project contracts.

For example, let's say the objective is to build an automobile. In New Democracy various specialty meshes of projects, a proxy, will bid on larger projects like contributing to an automobile. Collections of projects become a proxy for proxy voting. There are no hierarchies in New Democracy so no one specific MGO rules anything. All that exists are projects. Smaller projects are voting blocks to be included in larger projects of interest.

In New Democracy corruption voting has a different impact than in today's hierarchy voting where individuals are voted upon. No individuals are voted upon in New Democracy and neither are individual MGOs unless a project is small enough where single MGOs make sense. In New Democracy large projects collectively will have needs for smaller projects that outstrip the combined capacity of smaller projects. Corruption voting then is how larger projects compete for limited smaller project resources.

Proxy voting is mesh voting. A project is a proxy which is a collection of nested projects and MGOs.

All services and products in New Democracy are projects.

Vote Banking: Thinking in New Democracy 

In New Democracy pieces of innovation combined are themselves an innovation and not an ideology. There is nothing inherently good or bad by adopting some of all of a piece of engineering. One can adopt basic vote banking as well as adopt any of the additional innovations just listed. New Democracy is not an ideology. There is no ideal. New Democracy is about proven results. New Democracy is about experimenting with new social innovations the same way high tech has advanced by experimenting with tech innovations. Additional vote banking innovations can be introduced and do not represent or misrepresent New Democracy.

The differences between tech and social experiments is that tech is focused primarily on science where social innovations are primarily focused on what we want, our desires. No religion is founded upon experiments therefore it is to be expected that the religious community will reject experimental society because experimental society runs counter to ideological society.

Given in 2026 the vast majority of the world is religious, ideological society then New Democracy will necessarily be started as a small effort. A quest! This is also why New Democracy is a four-hundred-year plan. It will take at least twenty generations to dissolve religion.

Dissolve is the key word for innovation. Innovation is small increments. Innovation is not revolution. Innovation isn't one ideology conquering another. Innovation is simply the hard work of improving things in life one step at a time with small innovations of experiments.

New Democracy: what do we want? Old Democracy: what is ideal? 

New Democracy is the future. Old Democracy is the past where ideals of ideology where never ideals but instead just the wants of the few or the one imposed on the many.


